  Eleven of the twelve carry stub-sized method counts - 2, 4 or 8 methods each - which is what
  the shims artifact ships them as. The twelfth, org.apache.spark.unused.UnusedStubClass, is
  the marker class every Spark artifact carries; its 34 declarations are one per contributing
  archive and it is listed for completeness rather than as a stub measurement.

  THE OTHER SIDE OF THE COMPARISON IS OWNED ELSEWHERE, AND IS NOT RESTATED HERE AS THIS
  GRAPH'S: in the graph at the sanctioned path, whose input set EXCLUDES both shims archives,
  the same eleven classes carry full implementations (SparkConf 298, SparkContext 1,100,
  rdd.RDD 1,022, api.java.JavaRDD 74 and so on). That measurement is
  harness/artifacts/logs/cpg-shims-collision-measurement.log, taken first-hand against that
  graph. Reading the two together: in a graph that contains the shims artifact, the stub
  definitions displaced the real ones for those eleven classes. That is a recorded forward cost
  of the complete input set the AAP mandates - reported, not acted on, because the AAP forbids
  the exclusion that would avoid it.

